    "definition": {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "light",
        "align": "left",
        "width": "wide",
        "eyebrow": "",
        "heading": "A synbiotic for the gut-skin connection.",
        "heading_tag": "h2",
        "body": "<p>Skinbiotic+ combines a prebiotic with probiotic organisms in a single daily capsule. The prebiotic, organic inulin, is a fermentable fiber that can serve as food for certain intestinal microorganisms. The probiotic blend adds five organisms selected for their roles in gut ecology.</p><p>The formulation rationale is the pairing: fuel and organisms working together in the intestinal environment, where much of the conversation between gut and skin begins.</p>",
        "quote": "Prebiotic fuel. Probiotic organisms. One daily capsule."
      }
    },
    "video": {
      "type": "video-slot",
      "settings": {}
    },
    "axis": {
      "type": "flow-steps",
      "blocks": {
        "b1": {
          "type": "step",
          "settings": {
            "label": "Gut microbiome",
            "detail": "Trillions of microorganisms live in the intestinal tract, together called the gut microbiome."
          }
        },
        "b2": {
          "type": "step",
          "settings": {
            "label": "Microbial metabolites",
            "detail": "When gut microbes ferment dietary fiber, they produce compounds such as short-chain fatty acids that take part in normal immune and metabolic signaling."
          }
        },
        "b3": {
          "type": "step",
          "settings": {
            "label": "Immune signaling",
            "detail": "A large share of the body's immune tissue sits along the digestive tract, and immune messengers travel throughout the body."
          }
        },
        "b4": {
          "type": "step",
          "settings": {
            "label": "Systemic circulation",
            "detail": "Nutrients absorbed in the gut, and signaling compounds, reach the rest of the body through the bloodstream."
          }
        },
        "b5": {
          "type": "step",
          "settings": {
            "label": "Skin",
            "detail": "Skin relies on those nutrients and responds to that signaling. Researchers continue to study how much diet and supplements can influence it."
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "b1",
        "b2",
        "b3",
        "b4",
        "b5"
      ],
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "dark",
        "eyebrow": "",
        "heading": "What is the gut-skin connection?",
        "body": "<p>The gut-skin connection (or gut-skin axis) describes communication between the intestinal environment and the skin. Researchers study several pathways, including microbial metabolites, immune signaling, barrier integrity, and systemic metabolism.</p><p>The concept has substantial biological support. Outcomes still vary by organism, dose, and population, so we describe the research carefully and do not generalize it.</p>",
        "takeaway": "Skinbiotic+ is designed to support the gut side of that conversation.†"
      }
    },
    "synbiotic": {
      "type": "split-compare",
      "blocks": {
        "b1": {
          "type": "side",
          "settings": {
            "kicker": "Prebiotic",
            "name": "Organic inulin",
            "text": "A fermentable fiber that can serve as food for certain intestinal microorganisms."
          }
        },
        "b2": {
          "type": "side",
          "settings": {
            "kicker": "Probiotic",
            "name": "Five organisms",
            "text": "Lactobacillus plantarum, Bifidobacterium lactis, Lactobacillus rhamnosus, Bifidobacterium bifidum, and Akkermansia muciniphila."
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "b1",
        "b2"
      ],
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "green",
        "eyebrow": "",
        "heading": "What makes it a synbiotic?",
        "body": "<p>A synbiotic pairs a prebiotic with probiotics. Each side has a different job, and the formula is built around having both.</p>",
        "center": "Prebiotic + probiotic = synbiotic."
      }
    },
